        "t2": {
          "issueOrPullRequest": {
            "__typename": "PullRequest",
            "id": "PR_kwDOErHI988AAAABJ3wT1w",
            "number": 1611,
            "title": "fix(node): don't listen for close/error after a synchronous next()",
            "body": "### 🔗 Linked issue\n\nResolves #1610\n\n### ❓ Type of change\n\n- [x] 🐞 Bug fix (a non-breaking change that fixes an issue)\n\n### 📚 Description\n\n`callNodeListener` runs the handler first and only then registers `res.once(\"close\", next)` and `res.once(\"error\", next)`. A Node middleware that calls `next()` synchronously has already run `next`, and its `res.off(...)` found nothing to remove. The listeners are added afterwards and never removed. Each such layer leaves one `close` and one `error` listener on the response, so with more than 10 layers Node prints `MaxListenersExceededWarning`, as in the issue.\n\n`next` now sets a `called` flag, and the listeners are registered only if the handler returned without calling it. Everything else is unchanged:\n- a middleware that calls `next()` later still has its listeners, so `close` or `error` still settles it;\n- they are still removed when `next()` runs;\n- a returned value still resolves the promise.\n\nThis is against `v1`, since the report is for 1.15.11. `main`'s rewritten Node adapter doesn't register these per-middleware listeners, so it doesn't need the change. PR #1028 touched the same ordering for hanging responses; this change only stops the late registration.\n\nTest: in `test/app.test.ts`, 15 `fromNodeMiddleware((req, res, next) => next())` layers run before a final one that records `res.listenerCount(\"close\")` and `(\"error\")`. It expects `[0, 0]`, and on `v1` it fails. `vitest --run` passes all 356 tests (1 skipped), and `eslint`, `prettier -c` and `tsgo --noEmit` are clean (Node 22, offline).\n",
            "state": "OPEN",
            "isDraft": false,
            "createdAt": "2026-10-21T19:46:55Z",
